 "answer": "I can't get a live feed, so these are the latest readings from search results. They differ somewhat, and the pages may be cached.\n\n**Most recent reading (AQI.in, updated 2026-10-16, 15:51 local time):**\n- The US AQI is 180, which is \"Unhealthy\". PM2.5 is 111 µg/m³, PM10 is 151 µg/m³, and the temperature is 29.5°C.\n- Humidity is 70% and wind speed is 8.6 km/h.\n\n**Other sources:**\n- Another page lists Johor Bahru's AQI at 181, with Pasir Gudang at 183 and Parit Sulong at 182. That is consistent with the reading above.\n- SemakAPI, which uses official Malaysian API station data, shows Johor Bahru as Unhealthy, with an estimated PM2.5 of about 136.8 µg/m³ on a 24-hour average (roughly US AQI 212, Very Unhealthy). It also flags that data as stale.\n- An IQAir page showed a \"Moderate\" reading of 62 for Johor Bahru. That page looks cached from an earlier date, so I wouldn't rely on it.\n\n**Summary:** Air quality in Johor Bahru appears to be **Unhealthy** (US AQI of about 180). At that level, everyone may start to feel health effects, and sensitive groups may feel more serious ones. If you're going out, consider limiting prolonged outdoor exertion and wearing an N95 mask. Sensitive groups should be especially careful. These include children, older adults, and people with asthma or heart conditions.\n\nFor the exact current number, check the official Malaysian DOE API portal (apims.doe.gov.my), IQAir, or aqicn.org/city/johor-bahru.",
